WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.027 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A shopper buys tomato soup. Imagine

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:06.669 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
that the receipt says chicken noodle.
Did the camera miss the flavor?

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:09.370 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Amazon's Just Walk Out uses cameras

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.381 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and shelf sensors to follow
the items shoppers take.

00:00:12.781 --> 00:00:17.879 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Researchers can study a shopping trip
inside a simulated store, like Sari Sandbox.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:23.221 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Virtual try-on shows shoppers how a pair
of glasses would look on their own face.

00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:27.094 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We studied a checkout model
that recognized the soup brand

00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:28.784 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and still chose the wrong flavor.

00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:34.364 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We present A Transferable Variant Signal
That Grocery Retrieval Cannot Reach.

00:00:35.144 --> 00:00:40.203 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Look at this can. It is Campbell's Creamy
Tomato. The other can is Chicken Noodle.

00:00:40.603 --> 00:00:45.959 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A checkout model turns the photo and each
catalog description into a list of numbers.

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:50.227 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It compares the lists and ranks the
descriptions. This is product retrieval.

00:00:50.627 --> 00:00:54.020 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For the tomato soup photo,
Chicken Noodle ranked first.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.772 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model got the brand
right and the flavor wrong.

00:00:57.172 --> 00:01:00.445 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An earlier grocery retrieval
study found the correct

00:01:00.445 --> 00:01:03.370 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
item was often among the first five choices,

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:04.895 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
even when it did not rank first.

00:01:05.675 --> 00:01:08.853 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Did the image model miss the
difference between the two soups?

00:01:09.473 --> 00:01:13.244 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Or did it record the difference,
then choose the wrong description?

00:01:13.244 --> 00:01:17.148 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We can separate those possibilities
by testing the image on its own.

00:01:18.528 --> 00:01:23.387 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We kept the image model fixed. Each photo
produced the same numbers as before.

00:01:23.787 --> 00:01:25.988 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Using photos with known labels,

00:01:25.988 --> 00:01:29.691 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
we taught a simple classifier how
much weight to give each number.

00:01:29.691 --> 00:01:34.187 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This test is called a linear probe. It
never reads the catalog descriptions.

00:01:34.587 --> 00:01:36.763 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On new photos of the training brands,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:42.005 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
the probe reached 97.1 percent
balanced accuracy with SigLIP2.

00:01:42.005 --> 00:01:44.715 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each variety counted equally within its brand.

00:01:45.115 --> 00:01:49.440 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
So the image features already contained
information about the variety.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.680 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The ordinary match to text could miss it.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.697 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Could a distinction learned from
one brand help with another?

00:01:55.697 --> 00:01:58.727 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We began with brands for
which we had labeled photos.

00:01:59.347 --> 00:02:02.861 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For each variety, we averaged
its photo measurements.

00:02:02.861 --> 00:02:06.867 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The arrow runs from the brand
average to the variety average.

00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:09.619 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It shows how that variety
differs from its brand.

00:02:10.239 --> 00:02:13.540 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now imagine laying a ruler along those arrows.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.841 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Along some directions the
varieties spread apart.

00:02:16.841 --> 00:02:20.868 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We kept 64 directions that
captured the most variation.

00:02:20.868 --> 00:02:23.001 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Together they form the variant subspace.

00:02:23.621 --> 00:02:27.499 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The calculation is called
singular value decomposition.

00:02:27.499 --> 00:02:32.437 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
V gives the directions in the image
features. Sigma gives their strengths.

00:02:32.437 --> 00:02:35.723 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
U shows how each variety
contributes to each direction.

00:02:36.503 --> 00:02:39.075 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To check whether the variety names mattered,

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:41.452 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
we repeated the calculation with one change.

00:02:41.852 --> 00:02:45.245 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We shuffled the labels among
photos of the same brand.

00:02:45.245 --> 00:02:48.852 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A tomato photo could now
carry a chicken noodle label.

00:02:48.852 --> 00:02:52.521 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The photos stayed in place, and
each name appeared just as often.

00:02:52.921 --> 00:02:54.898 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We learned a second set of directions

00:02:54.898 --> 00:02:58.384 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from these shuffled labels.
The test labels stayed correct.

00:02:59.164 --> 00:03:02.643 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now take a brand we left out
when learning the directions.

00:03:02.643 --> 00:03:05.373 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Here the products are three
Starbucks coffee roasts.

00:03:05.773 --> 00:03:10.639 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We project each coffee photo and each
description onto the same directions.

00:03:10.639 --> 00:03:13.733 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It is like measuring both
with the same set of rulers.

00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:17.296 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Using measurements learned from
other brands is subspace transfer.

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:19.959 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Look at the ten photos of Sumatra.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:23.757 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original training labels,
nine matched the correct roast.

00:03:23.757 --> 00:03:26.979 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With shuffled training labels,
all ten matched Pike Place.

00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:32.233 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
One roast is only one example.
We repeated the test ten times,

00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:34.609 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
setting aside different brands each time.

00:03:34.609 --> 00:03:39.757 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Across all nine models, the correct catalog
entry ranked first more often on average

00:03:39.757 --> 00:03:41.009 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
with the original labels.

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:43.480 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Where did that advantage come from?

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.007 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For SigLIP2 it was 13.6 percentage
points across the whole catalog.

00:03:49.007 --> 00:03:53.295 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
When every choice came from the
correct brand, it fell to 3.9 points.

00:03:53.695 --> 00:03:56.296 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On the two models we examined closely,

00:03:56.296 --> 00:03:59.349 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
most of the advantage came
from telling brands apart.

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:01.722 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The full image features still gave higher

00:04:01.722 --> 00:04:05.259 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
overall retrieval accuracy
than the smaller subspace.

00:04:06.039 --> 00:04:10.572 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Return to the tomato soup. The
checkout model chose Chicken Noodle.

00:04:10.572 --> 00:04:13.198 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Yet a simple classifier could read the variety

00:04:13.198 --> 00:04:16.076 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from the image features the model already had.

00:04:16.696 --> 00:04:20.936 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Some of those distinctions helped
identify coffee from a new brand.

00:04:20.936 --> 00:04:25.347 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Across the catalog, most of the
gain was in recognizing the brand.

00:04:25.347 --> 00:04:28.594 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To test whether a checkout
model recognizes a flavor,

00:04:28.594 --> 00:04:30.936 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
give it several flavors of the same brand.

00:04:31.556 --> 00:04:32.857 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Thank you for listening.
